WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.330
- Where did you get cadavers?

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:02.900
- I don't blame you guys around
understanding cause you guys

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.980
haven't seen the amount of Tai
Lopez courses that I've seen.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.320
- Ha, he said Tai Lopez!

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.760
I've made a video reacting

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.230
to unprofessional medical TikToks.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.063
How about funny ones?

00:00:12.063 --> 00:00:13.530
I'm sure there's some
funny ones out there.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.903
Let's go searching, pew!

00:00:14.903 --> 00:00:15.942
- This is my first time.

00:00:15.942 --> 00:00:18.440
I can't, I don't know how to do this.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.239
It's, ooh!

00:00:20.239 --> 00:00:22.470
Golly, I ain't trying to do that!

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.694
- This is questionable.

00:00:23.694 --> 00:00:26.025
- (laughs) "This is questionable."

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:27.530
Honestly, the first time
you do an ultrasound,

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.440
it is confusing.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.980
I can relate to that struggle.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.450
That jelly, just so cold when it hits

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:33.283
the bare skin.
(jelly squelches)

00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:34.116
Urgh.

00:00:34.116 --> 00:00:36.463
- Doctor, something's wrong with me!

00:00:36.463 --> 00:00:38.220
- Explain to me what's wrong.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.289
- When I touch my head, it hurts.

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:41.988
When I touch my nose, it hurts.

00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:44.320
When I touch my elbow, it hurts.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.810
- I now see your problem.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.970
- Oh no, am I gonna die?

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.710
- No, your fingers broken.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.050
- Ha ha, I did not expect that!

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.650
People are so much wittier than I am.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.460
That's so good, I thought
she was gonna make a joke

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.410
about fibromyalgia, and
I was gonna get upset,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.800
like you shouldn't make fun of patients,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.720
and she said your finger (laughs) hurts.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.610
That's really good.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.310
Also, there's a play on that doctor's joke

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.277
where it's like, "Doctor,
hurts when I do this."

00:01:06.277 --> 00:01:08.560
"Oh okay, don't do this, copay."

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.490
- Hi, I'm Dr. Jones.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.680
I'm an ob-gyn physician.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.700
I'll tell you what I did today,
but you have to listen fast.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.100
I put on a mask because COVID.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.510
I got some coffee because
I'm addicted to caffeine.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.010
After that, I went to the operating room

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.850
and did two surgeries,
one major, one minor,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.370
before having socially-distanced chips

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.680
for lunch and doing an HSG.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.740
I'll tell you what that is another day.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.530
I went to my clinic and saw patients,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.710
pregnant and not pregnant,
and then I went to my office

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.350
and I (inhales) charted forever,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.810
and then I went to labor and delivery,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.100
and we delivered a baby,
and it was super fun,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.890
and now I'm going home
because I'm exhausted.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.540
- Mama Doctor Jones is a champ,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.060
not only because of everything
she does in her life

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.230
and doing it quite well.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.760
She's amazing at TikTok.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.050
It's a really good representation

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.570
of how hard it is to be a doctor,

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.570
especially one who's seeing patients

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.910
in a clinic setting,
but also is a surgeon.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.010
It's not an easy life.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.290
Mama Doctor Jones is up to the challenge.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.840
If you haven't seen our collab,
I'm linking it down below.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.300
- But sorry, bro, your daddy's dead.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.437
- Aw man!

00:01:58.437 --> 00:01:59.422
- Calm down, man.

00:01:59.422 --> 00:02:00.775
I'm the one that should be upset.

00:02:00.775 --> 00:02:02.180
I had to go on break right after.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.140
You know how hard it
is to eat a sloppy joe

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.080
after seeing blood?

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.010
- Did he go quick?

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.150
- Oh nah, he took his time.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.100
I'm like, "Hurry up, I'm
trying to go on break."

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.877
He fighting, I'm like, "Let go, man,

00:02:10.877 --> 00:02:13.130
"you got God on hold, man."

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:13.970
- Did he suffer?

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.120
- Oh, most definitely
'cause he was screaming.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.400
I'm like, "Man, this is annoying"

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.360
'cause I know the other
patient's trying to sleep.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.030
I'm on the phone trying to
talk to my insurance agency,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.100
trying to get my insurance
lower, but he yelling,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.930
they can't hear me, I'm like, "Shut up."

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.470
If it makes you feel better,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.430
I'm sure Mr. Johnson is in a better--.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.460
- Wait, my dad's last name is Wilson.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.110
- Oh, your last name Wilson?

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.020
Aw, my fault.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.630
Your daddy straight, brah, he outside.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:37.930
Hey, you Mr. Johnson son?

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.290
- Yeah.
- Your daddy's dead.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.123
- Oh, thank you.

00:02:40.123 --> 00:02:41.560
- You welcome.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.750
- That was rough.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.730
I have to say

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.720
name mistakes in hospitals do happen.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.710
This is a real issue.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.280
In fact, when we have two people

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.770
either with the same last name

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.460
or a last name that sounds similar,

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.980
we put on our floors name alerts.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.230
It says name alert above the patient's bed

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.310
and when you enter the room

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.330
so that these mistakes don't
happen, and when people

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.980
are creating the person's chart,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.390
it has a name alert on it.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:06.790
It's like a bright sticker

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.780
so that you're checking
the right person's name.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.970
- Hey, doctor, what's that thing?

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.390
- Great question, young man.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.240
That would be the heart.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.620
- Hey, doc--
- Scalpel.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:16.530
- Of course.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:17.540
Doctor, I was wondering

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.570
if that was the right coronary artery.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.120
- No, that is a circumflex artery.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.060
Get out of the OR.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.840
- Some doctors, especially surgeons,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.640
can be quite mean to
residents and students,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.720
but they're really nice
to those who are shadowing

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.640
trying to decide whether or no
they wanna go into the field

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:32.950
because they don't wanna scare them away,

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.150
plus it's also fair to say that
they're under no obligation

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.440
to know all the important arteries.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.190
That person did ask what that
is, and it was the heart.

00:03:43.383 --> 00:03:44.970
Eh, the one thing that I will
say with quiet certainty,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.700
and it's all jokes aside,

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.830
is we need to end that toxic culture

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.900
of attacking residents and students,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.260
that if they don't know
something they're dumb

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.720
or they're evil or we
need to kick them out.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.240
That doesn't really work.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.960
It doesn't motivate you to do better.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.310
It actually kind of puts you
down and demotivates you.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:00.420
It's no good for anybody.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.010
- My Harvard days were my peak.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:02.900
God, those days were fun.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.340
- Dr. Welch, where'd you
go to medical school?

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.507
- I actually didn't go to school.

00:04:05.507 --> 00:04:06.340
Learned everything I know

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.861
through YouTube tutorials and eBooks.

00:04:07.861 --> 00:04:08.694
(doctors laugh)

00:04:08.694 --> 00:04:09.527
No, no, I'm serious.

00:04:09.527 --> 00:04:11.450
I literally don't have
a legal medical degree

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.020
'cause everything that I
was learning in school,

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.930
I was like, I can just
learn this on my own,

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:16.460
so that's what I did, I studied on my own,

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.940
and I worked on cadavers outta my garage.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.620
I'm like the Mark
Zuckerberg of doctor people.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.660
- Where did you get cadavers?

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.060
- And how are you the medical director

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.170
at a children's hospital?

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.520
It's illegal to operate without a license.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.040
- Is it illegal to save
a dying child's life?

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.210
- Yeah, for you it is.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.730
- Dr. Welch, where did you get cadavers?

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.000
- I don't blame you guys
for not understanding

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:30.970
'cause you guys haven't seen the amount

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.400
of Tai Lopez courses that I've seen.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.260
- Ha, he said, "Tai Lopez
courses," oh my God.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.920
Honestly, there's a lot
you can learn online,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.240
and I feel like I'm about
to release some merch soon,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.560
some limited edition merch,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.660
about how much education
you can get online,

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.330
specifically from watching my videos,

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.960
but so far, this is
hilarious, let's keep playing.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.150
- Knowledge will take you so far in life

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.260
without a college degree.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.250
For example, every child that I save

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.760
represents a new Lamborghini
that comes into my life,

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.040
which is why I have 43
Lamborghinis in my garage.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.720
- You took Tai Lopez courses

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.440
to learn how to be an open heart surgeon?

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.211
- Gary Vaynerchuk was also
a very large influence.

00:05:04.211 --> 00:05:05.044
(door crunches)

00:05:05.044 --> 00:05:06.300
- President's daughter
has a puncture aorta.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.110
You two, sit the (beep) down.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.070
Dr. Welch, get in that operating room

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.143
and save the president's daughter's life.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.600
- Ah yeah, medicine is
one of those things where,

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:17.940
why am I even explaining this?

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.210
Yeah, you can't practice
medicine without a license,

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.880
and it's not because the license

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.260
is some kind of magical paper

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.710
that actually proves you're capable,

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.750
but when you're about to trust
someone with your health,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:31.210
you hope that they've been
experienced, they've practice,

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.730
they've learned, they show
competency in these fields.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.700
So yeah, you want someone who's not only

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.930
showing you a medical school diploma,

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.300
but also a residency
graduation certificate,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.600
a board certification.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.500
I'm board certified in family medicine.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.360
I have a medical license
in New York and New Jersey.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.480
They ask you a lotta questions
about criminal backgrounds.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.130
Where did this guy get a cadaver?

00:05:51.777 --> 00:05:53.429
- Would you mind?

00:05:53.429 --> 00:05:54.390
(door knocks)

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.507
- Yeah.
- Your patient is ready.

00:05:55.507 --> 00:05:56.530
- All right, be there in a second.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.695
- Breath!
(doctor laughs)

00:05:58.695 --> 00:05:59.528
- There's one more part we can take--

00:05:59.528 --> 00:06:01.150
- [Man] Your patient is waiting.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.296
- Oh, my bad.

00:06:02.296 --> 00:06:05.870
- No, as funny as that is,
that's now what happens.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.010
Dude, if we sat and watched
SpongeBob or whatever

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.520
for 45 minutes before
going to see patients,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.380
we would be five hours
late to each patient.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.660
Think about it this way.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.120
I go into a room with a patient.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.190
Technically, I'm scheduled for 15 minutes.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.260
My nurse has to be in there
for the first five minutes

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:22.390
gathering the chief complaint,

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:25.080
asking some basic
questions, doing the vitals.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:25.960
That's five minutes.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.300
Now I have 10 minutes with the patient.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.940
Let's say a walk in exactly on time.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.620
I introduce myself to the patient.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.150
I ask them what they're here for.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:33.110
I ask them their name.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.160
I do a proper greeting to make sure

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.350
that we're on the same page.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.770
I ask them what's bothering them.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.420
I ask some follow up question.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.520
I figure out how long it's been an issue,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.920
what other things run in their family.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.620
I look at their prescription medications.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.360
I decide to do a physical exam,

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.960
do a few things to make sure
I'm ruling out conditions

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.760
that could be otherwise
threatening to their health

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.540
and to figure out what exactly
I think could be going on.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.250
I then sit onto the computer,
and I explained to them

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.520
what is going on.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.230
I explain to them my treatment plan.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:00.410
I make sure they understand.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.910
I have them repeat back to me
the same set of instructions

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.020
so we're on the same page.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.770
I then placed the orders in the computer

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.450
so that they have their medications ready

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.360
or treatment ready when
they leave the office.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.230
I then go back to my area,
waiting to see the next patient.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.720
How much time do you think has gone by?

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.600
I didn't even begin to
write this patient's note,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.830
and I can't really wait to
do it at the end of the day

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.040
'cause then I'll get home way
later than I'm supposed to

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.470
and everyone's mad because
I'm missing schedules,

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:24.920
I'm being late to the next patient,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.560
and it creates a huge hurdle.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.120
The amount of administrative work

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.910
that doctors now have to do

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.110
is beyond anyone's imagination,

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:34.120
like spending 30 minutes making sure

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.680
that my patient gets their medication

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.410
'cause I ordered capsules
instead of tablets

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.880
happens way too often.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:39.890
That's why we're late,

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:41.430
not because we're watching SpongeBob

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.120
although that is cuter and
funnier to think about.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.800
- All right, so, Chad,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.610
Um, it looks like you've a
minor tear in your bicep,

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.030
uh, nothing too severe,

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.570
but we're gonna have you
take about a month off

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.490
from any physical activities.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.030
Um, we'll bring ya back in,

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.550
we'll reevaluate you,
and then we'll clear you

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.490
based off of how well, um,
you've recovered, okay.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:01.490
Sounds good?

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:02.323
Awesome.

00:08:02.323 --> 00:08:04.150
- Ha, month off!

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.808
Does he not know who I am?

00:08:05.808 --> 00:08:06.891
Ha ha, idiot!

00:08:08.251 --> 00:08:09.084
Argh!

00:08:09.084 --> 00:08:10.850
- The funny part is I love
that he's a Bengals fan.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.340
Shout out Cincinnati folks.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.280
This happens a lot.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.800
Most commonly, where it's happened

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.530
is around sporting events,

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.750
like if someone's training
for the New York City Marathon

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.370
and they've come in with a serious injury,

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.340
sometimes even though with
something really serious

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.637
like a stress fracture,
and I'm like, "Do not run

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:27.087
"under any circumstances

00:08:27.087 --> 00:08:29.770
"'cause you could fracture
